    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the root-scarred sounds cool. i like the idea of them being a primal threat. can you go deeper into what they actually look like? like, if a hero from the empire saw one of them for the first time, what physical traits would stand out to them as being \"uncivilized\" or \"other\"? i want their physical appearance to really scream that they come from a different, more untamed part of the world.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To make the physical contrast stark, imagine their skin textured like rough bark or living moss, often covered in natural pigments that pulse with the seasons rather than painted with uniform rivers. Unlike the glowing lights or sleek gowns of the empire, their bodies seem uneven and asymmetrical, bearing permanent scars that they treat as sacred tattoos rather than injuries to be hidden. Their eyes might lack color entirely or hold multiple hues like fractured glass, allowing them to feel vibrations in the earth that smooth-skinned subjects cannot perceive. Relying on biology over man-made tools, they wear masks crafted from hollowed bone or waterproof leather, emphasizing their rejection of the polished surfaces that define the hero society. This visceral, shifting display of \"living material\" should shock your readers into realizing the horror of a world where nature feels like a prison, not a home.",
